ges numbered 1 through 50 has its pages renumbered in reverse, from 50 to 1. For how many pages do both sets of page numbers share the same ones digit?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"85911905485e90de7a2e55d60a6a0eeea777b69fe81623cab7eb4fb2fbd79cff"},"seconds":59.08933401107788,"seed":259602150069561095,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to determine how many pages have the same ones digit when the page numbers are renumbered from 50 to 1 in reverse order.\n\n1. **Identify the original and new page numbering scheme:**\n   - Original numbering: 1, 2, 3, ..., 50.\n   - New numbering (reverse order): 50, 49, 48, ..., 1.\n\n2. **Determine the ones digit for each page in both numbering schemes:**\n   - Original page number: \\( n \\) (where \\( n \\) ranges from 1 to 50).\n   - New page number: \\( 51 - n \\).\n\n3. **Compare the ones digit of \\( n \\) and \\( 51 - n \\):**\n   - The ones digit of \\( n \\) is \\( n \\mod 10 \\).\n   - The ones digit of \\( 51 - n \\) is \\( (51 - n) \\mod 10 \\).\n\n4. **Set up the equation for the ones digits to be equal:**\n   \\[\n   n \\mod 10 = (51 - n) \\mod 10\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve the equation:**\n   - Let \\( n \\mod 10 = x \\), where \\( x \\) is the ones digit of \\( n \\).\n   - Then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 1 - x \\) (since \\( 51 \\mod 10 = 1 \\)).\n   - Therefore, we need \\( x = 1 - x \\).\n\n6. **Solve for \\( x \\):**\n   \\[\n   2x = 1 \\implies x = 0.5\n   \\]\n   - Since \\( x \\) must be an integer (ones digit), there is no integer solution for \\( x \\).\n\n7. **Re-evaluate the problem:**\n   - We need to find \\( n \\) such that the ones digit of \\( n \\) and \\( 51 - n \\) are the same.\n   - Consider the possible values of \\( n \\mod 10 \\) and check if they match \\( (51 - n) \\mod 10 \\).\n\n8. **Check each possible ones digit from 0 to 9:**\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 0 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 1 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 1 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 0 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 2 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 9 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 3 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 8 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 4 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 7 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 5 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 6 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 6 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 5 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 7 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 4 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 8 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 3 \\).\n   - If \\( n \\mod 10 = 9 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 2 \\).\n\n9. **Identify the valid cases:**\n   - The only valid case is when \\( n \\mod 10 = 5 \\), then \\( 51 - n \\mod 10 = 6 \\).\n\n10. **Count the valid pages:**\n    - Pages with ones digit 5: 5, 15, 25, 35, 45.\n    - There are 5 such pages.\n\nThus, the number of pages that have the same ones digit in both numbering schemes is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
